---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Rule from "../components/Rule.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Roadmap"
      eyebrow="What is left, in the order it is worth doing"
      dek="The question this archive was built to answer has been answered. This page says what replaced it, ranks the ways at the new one, and marks plainly which routes are finished, which are live, and which need a person rather than a search.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The question that started it, and its answer</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Who were Pablo Armando Lerena&rsquo;s parents?</strong> For most of this archive&rsquo;s
      life that was the whole of it. The line below him was closed and documented; above him there was
      nothing at all, and the Argentine record refused three times over.</p>
    <p>It was answered by a document he filled in himself. His <strong>1908 Argentine army enrolment
      card</strong> names them: <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> and <strong>María Lerena</strong>.
      Corroborated by his eldest son being christened <strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos</strong>, and
      independently by a granddaughter&rsquo;s DNA, which carries a Río de la Plata genetic group that can
      only have come through him.</p>
    <p class="cap">It is also the archive&rsquo;s worst near-miss. The card was
      <strong>written off for a month</strong> on four objections, three of which turned out to be
      artefacts of a machine index. Opening the image took two minutes.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>The correction &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="The question that replaced it" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Who were Juan Carlos and María Lerena, and where did they come from?</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Two names on a card, and nothing behind them. <strong>No baptism, no marriage, no census entry, no
      death</strong> for either of them has been found anywhere. And the reason that mattered so much on
      13 September is that the last place they could plausibly have hidden was checked, and they are not
      in it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario baptism index, 1879&ndash;1901.</strong> End to end. No Lerena in
        any spelling, and no Pablo Armando under any surname in 1881, 1882 or 1883.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario baptism index, 1731&ndash;1879.</strong> Eighty openings. The
        surname occurs <strong>once</strong>, and she is Uruguayan.</p></div>
    <div class="panel"><span class="chip doc">Read in full</span>
      <p class="ab"><strong>The Rosario marriage index, 1867&ndash;1885.</strong> No Lerena. They did not
        marry in that parish.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px"><strong>That is the whole surviving parish record of the city,
    a hundred and seventy years of it, and the family is not in any of it.</strong> A household that lives
    in a parish leaves baptisms. This one leaves nothing &mdash; which is no longer a gap in the searching.
    It is a fact about the family.</div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="The answer that now leads" />

<section class="blk">
  <h2>They were not a Rosario family &mdash; and the one Lerena in that parish was Uruguayan</h2>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Strong lead, no link proved &mdash; 13 September 2026</span></p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>The single Lerena baptism in the whole index, <strong>folio 229, February 1866</strong>, was opened
      the same day it was found. The act says of both parents:
      <em><strong>&ldquo;naturales del E. Oriental&rdquo;</strong></em> &mdash; natives of Uruguay. The
      father is a <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>, the mother a <strong>Juanicó</strong>, and the
      godfather &mdash; another Don Luis Lerena &mdash; stood <strong>by proxy</strong>, which is what one
      does from the other side of the river.</p>
    <p>And the family that produces is one this archive had been circling from the opposite direction for
      four days: the sons of <strong>Luis Lerena y Fernández</strong> and <strong>Justiniana Lenguas
      González</strong> of Montevideo, one of whom is <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong>,
      co-founder of the <strong>Argentine Stud Book</strong> &mdash; already in the register, marked
      <em>high interest</em>, because <strong>Pablo Armando trained and owned racehorses</strong>.</p>
    <p><strong>Nothing links Juan Carlos Lerena to that house.</strong> But it explains four separate
      refusals at once, and it says where to look.
      <a href={u("/hypotheses/")}>Hypothesis 1c &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<Rule name="arrows" label="Routes, ranked by what they would settle" />

<section class="blk">
  <h3 style="margin-top:8px">1 · The Montevideo registers <span class="chip inf" style="margin-left:8px">worked once, and the indexes came back empty</span></h3>
  <p>Run on the evening of 13 September, once FamilySearch let this session back in. The
    <strong>indexed</strong> Uruguayan record was searched for the man himself: there is
    <strong>no Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> born in Uruguay between 1830 and 1870 in
    <em>Catholic Church Records 1726&ndash;2000</em>, in <em>Civil Registration 1879&ndash;2020</em>, or in
    the <em>Baptisms 1750&ndash;1900</em> index; and <strong>no Lerena groom marrying a María</strong> there
    between 1865 and 1885. FamilySearch&rsquo;s <strong>full-text</strong> search &mdash; which reads
    handwriting across un-indexed images, and which <em>does</em> cover Montevideo &mdash; returns exactly
    <strong>two</strong> hits for the phrase &ldquo;Juan Carlos Lerena&rdquo; in the entire corpus, both
    twentieth-century and neither ours.</p>
  <p><strong>What is left is the reading.</strong> Full text does not cover everything, and an index is
    not a register. The Montevideo parishes &mdash; the Matriz, San Francisco de Asís, the Cordón &mdash;
    hold <strong>marriage registers for the 1870s</strong> that have to be turned page by page, the way
    Rosario was. The target is a <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena marrying a María before 1882</strong>. The
    Arquidiócesis <strong>marriage-dispensation register, 1856&ndash;1932</strong>, is the shortest way in:
    if she was a Lerena by birth, the marriage needed a dispensation, and dispensations are a thin volume
    rather than a parish&rsquo;s worth of acts.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">2 · The consulate that issued the passport <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">letter sent, 9 Sep 2026 — no reply yet</span></h3>
  <p>To be issued an Argentine passport in 1940 he had to prove nationality, so the Cape Town consulate
    held a <em>matrícula consular</em> on him, and those files went home to the <strong>Archivo Histórico
    de Cancillería</strong>. Sent to <span class="mono">archivo@cancilleria.gob.ar</span>, an address taken
    from the Cancillería&rsquo;s own contact page rather than guessed. It asks whether the matrícula
    survives, whether the 1940 passport file survives and <strong>whether it names his parents</strong>,
    and &mdash; flagged as minor &mdash; whether he ever held a consular appointment, which puts the
    family&rsquo;s Consul General tradition to the only body that could settle it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">3 · The rest of his own army file <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">letter sent, with the card attached</span></h3>
  <p>The 1908 enrolment card is an index card. Behind it sits a <em>legajo</em> &mdash; the service file
    &mdash; which would carry his birthplace, his parents&rsquo; details in full, and quite possibly his
    parents&rsquo; own origins. Written to the <strong>Archivo General del Ejército</strong> with the card
    itself attached, so they have the reference number rather than a description of it.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">4 · He came with the horses <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">untried, and still the biggest idea here</span></h3>
  <p>We have been hunting a <strong>passenger</strong>. He may never have been one. The South African War
    shipped some <strong>360,000 horses</strong> into the country, <strong>Argentina among the main
    sources</strong>. He was <strong>seventeen when it began and twenty when it ended</strong>, a horseman
    by trade and a horse-dealer at the Cape by 1911. Horses travel with <strong>conductors, grooms and
    muleteers</strong>, engaged at the port of loading, and those men are written down in
    <strong>military remount paperwork, not civilian passenger lists</strong> &mdash; which would explain
    why he is invisible in every passenger index searched.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px">Where: <strong>The National Archives, Kew &mdash; WO 108</strong> and related
    Remount Department series; the Cape remount depots, chiefly <strong>Stellenbosch</strong>; and the
    British purchasing commissions at Buenos Aires. <strong>None of it has been touched.</strong></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">5 · The 1935 mission census <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">needs a person in Brisbane</span></h3>
  <p>Both younger sons appear in an <strong>LDS South African Mission census of 1935</strong>, each indexed
    as <strong>&ldquo;Son&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; which means a <strong>head of household</strong> stands on
    that page, and it should be Pablo Armando, giving his own birthplace <strong>five years before the
    passport and independent of it</strong>. The image is not online; it needs a reader at an affiliate
    library. Why two Catholic boys of eleven and fifteen are in a Mormon mission census in the year their
    mother died is a question in itself.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">6 · The Ley 4144 expulsion files <span class="chip lore" style="margin-left:8px">needs David, and a passport</span></h3>
  <p>The family tradition is that he left Argentina as a political refugee. The Ley 4144 of 1902 deported
    <em>foreigners</em>, which is the flaw in the story and also the test of it. The
    <strong>Archivo General de la Nación</strong> portal needs an account; the Spanish text is written and
    ready to paste. <a href={u("/errands/")}>The errands &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:24px">7 · Rosario, again <span class="chip doc" style="margin-left:8px">closed — do not reopen without a new reason</span></h3>
  <p>Both volumes of the baptism index read end to end. 1881, 1882 and 1883 read across
    <strong>all twenty-six letter sections</strong> for a <em>Pablo Armando</em> under any surname
    whatsoever &mdash; roughly two hundred image-reads, more than twenty near misses logged and excluded,
    including the only two Armandos in three years. The marriage index read at full resolution. The 1895
    national census: not one Lerena in Santa Fe. <strong>This route is exhausted, not merely
    unsuccessful.</strong></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What would close it fastest</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p><strong>Route 1, as soon as FamilySearch lets this session back in.</strong> It costs nothing but
      time, it is the first place the evidence now points, and it is the only route that could name Juan
      Carlos Lerena&rsquo;s own parents rather than merely confirm his existence.</p>
    <p><strong>Routes 2 and 3 are already out</strong> and can only be waited on. If both come back empty,
      <strong>route 4</strong> is where the real thinking has to go &mdash; because if he came to Africa
      with a cargo of horses rather than a ticket, then no passenger list will ever have him, and we have
      been looking in the wrong archive from the beginning.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">Nothing here unseats the passport. It is his own document,
    sworn at a consulate, naming a city, a province and a day &mdash; and the enrolment card, filled in
    thirty-two years earlier, agrees with it. What the parish record refuses to do is corroborate either
    one. An archive that says so plainly is worth more than one that quietly stops looking.</div>
</section>
</Base>
